# Commits and pull requests

## Commit messages

Write every commit subject as Conventional Commits with a scope:

`type(scope): subject`

- Types: `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `style`, `refactor`, `perf`, `test`, `build`, `ci`, `chore`, `revert`
- Subject is imperative, lowercase, and has no trailing period
- Put extra explanation in the commit body, not in the subject
- Do not put the pull request template, checkboxes, or markdown headings in the commit message

```
fix(regions): read the normalized loader URL
```

## Pull request descriptions

Hand-written pull request descriptions use `.github/pull_request_template.md`:

- **What & why:** the change and the reason, from the commit subject plus the commit body when there is one
- **Type:** check the one box that matches the prefix (`feat`, `fix`, `docs` / `style` / `refactor` / `perf` / `test`, or `build` / `ci` / `chore`)
- **Checklist:** leave every box unchecked until a person confirms it

```
## What & why

fix(regions): read the normalized loader URL

## Type

- [ ] feat
- [x] fix
- [ ] docs / style / refactor / perf / test
- [ ] build / ci / chore

## Checklist

- [ ] Commits follow Conventional Commits (`type(scope): subject`)
- [ ] `npm run lint && npm run typecheck && npm run build` pass locally
- [ ] Checked pages on mobile and desktop
- [ ] Copy/claims reviewed (no unverified stats, testimonials or partner claims)
```

Release pull requests (`dev` into `main`) stay a promote line plus the commit list.
